<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durgasimha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durgasimha was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> poet and minister of the Western Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-ii">Jayasimha II</a> in the early eleventh century. His Panchatantra, adapted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> sources in the champu style of mixed verse and prose, is one of the earliest versions of the Panchatantra fables in a regional Indian language and a classic of Kannada literature.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Durgasimha was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> by birth and belonged to the Smartha Bhagavata sub-sect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, a community that gives equal importance to the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. He was a native of Kisukadu Nadu, another name for ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, and lived at the Sayyadi agrahara, a Brahmin school of higher learning. At the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-ii">Jayasimha II</a>, also known as Jagadekamalla, who reigned from 1018 to 1042, he held the office of minister of war and peace, the Sandhi Vigrahi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Kannada Panchatantra contains 60 fables, 13 of them original stories, and its central theme has a strong Jain bent. Every story turns on morality and ends with a summary section, the Katha Shloka. As a minister, Durgasimha treated the fables as a book of political science, or Rajniti. The scholar R. Narasimhachar dated the work to about 1025, while the Kannada poet and scholar Govinda Pai placed it on 8 March 1031, from the concluding stanza of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>.</p><p>The original Sanskrit Panchatantra is lost; it was an independent Sanskrit work of around 300 CE, give or take a century or two, and its putative author Vishnu Sharma may be a story-telling figure rather than a real person. Durgasimha worked from the so-called Southern Panchatantra, a version close to the original that also gave rise to other Indian vernacular versions and to the Hitopadesha.</p><p>Durgasimha, or his source, gave the work an origin story of its own. In his account Pushpadatta, an attendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, overheard the god telling a great story to his consort Parvati; Gunadhya, a reincarnation of Pushpadatta and court poet of King Shalivahana, later set it down in Paishachi as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihatkatha">Brihatkatha</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> selection of five stories from it by Vasubhaga Datta gave the Panchatantra its name. Modern scholars hold that the original Brihatkatha did not include the Panchatantra, which a later version made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> or north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> seems to have inserted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Durgasimha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ranna, court poet of Tailapa II and Satyashraya, wrote the Gadayuddha and the Ajitapurana, <b>Durgasimha</b> produced a Kannada Panchatantra under Jayasimha II, and grammarians and prosodists such as Nagavarma II codified the language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
